  intro="Of all four charts here, this is the one where diet makes the biggest difference — because insulin resistance sits underneath the skin and hair changes, and eating patterns genuinely affect it."
>
  <div class="note">
    <h3>Why this one matters more</h3>
    <p>
      In PCOS, insulin resistance drives androgen excess, and androgen excess drives the acne, the
      facial hair and the scalp thinning. Improving insulin sensitivity therefore works on the
      cause rather than the symptom.
    </p>
    <p>
      This is not a weight loss chart. PCOS occurs at every body weight, and the eating pattern
      below helps regardless of whether weight loss is relevant for you.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>The principle, in one line</h2>

  <p>
    <strong>Keep carbohydrates, change their form and what they are eaten with.</strong> You do not
    need to give up roti, rice, dal or fruit. You need to slow down how fast they raise blood
    sugar — which is achieved by pairing them with protein, fat and fibre, and by choosing whole
    forms over refined.
  </p>

  <h2>What to build meals around</h2>

  <h3>Protein at every meal — the single most useful change</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Dal, rajma, chana, lobia, whole moong</li>
    <li>Paneer, tofu, soya chunks</li>
    <li>Curd and buttermilk</li>
    <li>Eggs, chicken, fish</li>
    <li>Peanuts, almonds, roasted chana as snacks</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Most Indian vegetarian breakfasts are almost entirely carbohydrate. Adding protein to
    breakfast specifically has a disproportionate effect on blood sugar across the whole day.
  </p>

  <h3>Carbohydrates in whole form</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Whole wheat roti rather than maida</li>
    <li>Millets — bajra, jowar, ragi — two or three times a week</li>
    <li>Brown rice, or white rice in smaller portions alongside more dal and sabzi</li>
    <li>Oats, dalia</li>
    <li>Whole fruit, never juice</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Fibre</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Vegetables at both main meals, cooked and raw</li>
    <li>Salad before the meal rather than alongside it — it blunts the glucose rise</li>
    <li>Whole dals with their skins</li>
    <li>Flaxseed and chia, a spoon a day</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Fats that help</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Walnuts, flaxseed, chia — omega-3</li>
    <li>Mustard oil, groundnut oil, ghee in reasonable quantity</li>
    <li>Nuts and seeds as snacks instead of biscuits</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What to reduce</h2>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Sugary tea and coffee.</strong> Four cups with two spoons each is a large daily sugar load</li>
    <li><strong>Cold drinks and packaged juice</strong></li>
    <li><strong>Maida</strong> — bakery items, biscuits, naan, samosa, white bread</li>
    <li><strong>Instant noodles and packaged snacks</strong></li>
    <li><strong>Eating a large carbohydrate meal alone</strong> — rice on its own, or tea and biscuits as a meal</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>A day, put together</h2>

  <table>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <th scope="row" style="width:26%">Early morning</th>
        <td>Soaked almonds and walnuts · methi water or plain water. Avoid tea on an empty stomach</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <th scope="row">Breakfast</th>
        <td>Besan or moong dal chilla with curd · or 2 eggs with whole wheat roti · or vegetable upma with peanuts and a bowl of curd. Protein is the point here</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <th scope="row">Mid-morning</th>
        <td>Buttermilk, or a whole fruit with a few nuts — fruit alone spikes faster</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <th scope="row">Lunch</th>
        <td>Salad first · 2 roti (bajra or jowar some days) · dal or rajma · a green sabzi · curd</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <th scope="row">Evening</th>
        <td>Roasted chana, sprouts, or paneer cubes. Tea with less sugar or none</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <th scope="row">Dinner</th>
        <td>Light and early where possible · roti or a small portion of rice · paneer, soya, chicken or fish · vegetables</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Beyond food</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Movement after meals.</strong> A ten to fifteen minute walk after lunch and dinner
      measurably reduces the post-meal glucose rise. This is one of the highest-value habits
      available and it costs nothing.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Resistance training.</strong> Muscle improves insulin sensitivity more reliably than
      cardio alone. Twice a week is enough to matter.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Sleep.</strong> Short sleep worsens insulin resistance directly. This is not
      peripheral advice.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What to be sceptical about</h2>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Keto and other very low carbohydrate diets.</strong> They can improve markers short term, and most people cannot sustain them within an Indian household. A sustainable moderate change beats a strict one abandoned in six weeks</li>
    <li><strong>Detox teas and PCOS "cure" supplements.</strong> No evidence</li>
    <li><strong>Inositol</strong> — has some supporting evidence for PCOS, unlike most supplements, but discuss it with your doctor rather than self-prescribing</li>
    <li><strong>Eliminating dairy or gluten entirely.</strong> No good evidence in PCOS unless you have a specific intolerance</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="note">
    <h3>What to expect, and when</h3>
    <p>
      Cycle regularity often improves within three to six months of consistent change. Acne
      typically responds in two to three months. Hair changes — both scalp thinning and facial
      hair — take longest, often six to twelve months, because hair cycles slowly. None of this
      replaces medical treatment; it makes medical treatment work better.
    </p>
  </div>
